**I've found a really easy, not too hacky, workaround for this.**

After digging around on this I finally found something that works well and isn't too hacky (thanks to @michaelbromley for his research on this).

The typeorm function that chooses which columns to use first checks to see if you have declared input columns to use (passed as the second argument to the `into()` function.

```typescript
protected getInsertedColumns(): ColumnMetadata[] {
    if (!this.expressionMap.mainAlias!.hasMetadata)
        return [];

        return this.expressionMap.mainAlias!.metadata.columns.filter(column => {

            // if user specified list of columns he wants to insert to, then we filter only them
            if (this.expressionMap.insertColumns.length)
                return this.expressionMap.insertColumns.indexOf(column.propertyPath) !== -1;

            // skip columns the user doesn't want included by default
            if (!column.isInsert) { return false; }

            // if user did not specified such list then return all columns except auto-increment one
            // for Oracle we return auto-increment column as well because Oracle does not support DEFAULT VALUES expression
            if (column.isGenerated && column.generationStrategy === "increment"
                && !(this.connection.driver instanceof OracleDriver)
                && !(this.connection.driver instanceof AbstractSqliteDriver)
                && !(this.connection.driver instanceof MysqlDriver)
                && !(this.connection.driver instanceof AuroraDataApiDriver))
                return false;

            return true;
    });
} 
```

Using this, you can get all the columns for your entity and the resulting query will also include the ids. Or alternatively, you could just add whichever columns you want to use as the second argument for the `into()` method. This example is working for me:

```typescript

// If you want to get all of the columns

const vesselSnapshotLocationColumns = (await getConnection())
    .getMetadata(VesselSnapshotLocation)
    .ownColumns.map((column) => column.propertyName);

await queryBuilder
    .insert()
    .into(VesselSnapshotLocation, vesselSnapshotLocationColumns)
    .values(snapshotPositions)
    .onConflict("DO NOTHING")
    .execute();

// OR if you just want some of the columns.

await queryBuilder
    .insert()
    .into(VesselSnapshot, ["id", "time"])
    .values(snapshots)
    .onConflict("DO NOTHING")
    .execute();

```

@jordansoltman solution kind of worked for me. It does insert data with your id correctly set but postgres sequence did not get updated as expected for me.

If you need to reset the postgres id sequence, I added something like:

```javascript
  await MyModelRepository.query(
    `SELECT setval(
      pg_get_serial_sequence('my_model', 'id'),
      COALESCE((SELECT MAX(id) FROM my_model), 1)
    );`
  );
```

